WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.490
Bismillah alhamdulillah salatu wassalamu al
rasulullah. Hello Calculus students, my

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.830
name is Ziyad and today we're going to be
taking derivatives using our power rule.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.920
So let's dive right into some of the examples.
For our first equation, we have f of

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.600
x equals x cubed minus x squared plus x.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:24.020
And here, there are three terms that take on
the form of f of x equals x to

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:29.520
the power of n. So we can apply the power rule
three times, one time for each of

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:34.070
these three terms. So here, it would
start by taking f prime of x,

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:39.430
and we'd apply a power rule to our first
term. We have here n equals 3.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:45.470
So we can bring that 3 in front of
our x and subtract 1 from

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.870
3. It's going to be 3x here, 3
minus 1. Again, our 3

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.710
is our n, so we're taking that 3, we're
putting it in front of the x, and we're

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:58.140
subtracting 1 from 3. For our next term, we're
going to do minus, and then same

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.440
thing. We have here x squared. x squared would
be the same thing as bringing that 2

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:06.920
in front. It would become 2x, 2 minus 1. So
it's going to become 2x to the

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.060
2 minus 1.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.970
And then for our third term here, you might
think here we don't have any power for

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.070
x, but actually there's always an arbitrary power
of 1, right? Because any number to

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.550
the power of 1 is itself. If I have 5 to the
power of 1, it equals 5. 7 to the power

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.050
of 1 equals 7. So x to the power of 1
will also equal x. So here, if

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.250
we apply 1, x to the power of 1, in this power
rule, we're going to bring that 1 in

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.470
front, it's going to become 1,

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:39.870
times x to the 1 minus 1 because we have 1 as our
n and we're going to subtract 1 as

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:39.990
well.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.600
If we simplify this a little further, it's going
to be 3x to the power of 2 because

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:51.040
3 minus 1 is 2 minus 2x to the power of 1
because 2 minus 1 is 1 plus 1

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.780
times x to the 0.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:59.520
And now here we can have 3x squared again. We
said this one is arbitrary so we can

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:04.040
just write 2x. We don't need to write 2x to the
power of 1. And any number to the

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:08.980
power of 0 equals 1, right? If we have 10 to
the power of 0 equals 1, 20 to the

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:14.400
power of 0 equals 1, 2 gazillion to the power of
0 equals 1. So now we know that our

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.080
third term is going to become 1 times 1.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.940
And then that, of course, is going to simplify
to just 1. So our answer is going to

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:28.110
be 3x squared minus 2x plus 1. Now, to
our next example, we have

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.810
y equals 3x squared plus 5x plus 12.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.900
When we take the derivative of this, the only
thing that's going to really change is

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:41.180
because we have a factor at the beginning, we're
going to take that factor out and

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.360
then do our power rule in these brackets. We're
going to take that 3 out and then do

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.620
our power rule. And just like how we had x
squared here, we're going to do the exact

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.400
same thing. We're going to bring that 2 down and
subtract 1 from our power, so it's

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:58.680
going to become 2x to the power of 1. And then
here it's going to become plus 5 then

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.370
we're going to have our 1x to
the power of 0

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:07.590
and then lastly for our 12 and this
is a general rule for

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.370
any constant you run into a constant is a
number that doesn't have a variable

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.890
attached to it it's always going to go to 0 so
here because we have no variables

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:21.290
attached to our 12 and we're doing the
derivative with respect to x that means it's

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.850
going to go straight to 0 so it's going to
become instead of 12 just go right to

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:25.050
zero.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.690
Now, if we simplify this a little further, it's
going to become 2 times 3, which is

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.650
6. So it's going to become 6x plus 5 times

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.410
1 times x to the power of zero. And we know x to
the power of zero is 1. And 1 times

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:45.710
1 is 1. So it's just going to become 5. So our
answer is going to end up as 6x

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.980
plus 5. Now on to our next example. Here
we have h of x equals 6 over

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:55.760
x squared minus 4 over x cubed. Don't worry if
this looks a little bit different

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.440
than our first two examples. The only difference
we have to do here now is before we

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:05.040
take h prime of x and before we apply our
power rule, we just have to change a

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:10.340
little bit how h of x currently looks. And we can
do this by the following. Since we

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.820
know that 1 over x to the n
is the same as x

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:20.480
to the negative n, we can use this identity to
change these two so we don't have

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:25.860
these fractions anymore. So what we're going
to do here is, before we go to

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.940
h prime of x, we're going to stay at h of x and
turn this into 6 times x to the

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:36.340
negative 2 minus 4 times x to the
negative 3 and then now

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:41.780
we can take the power rule as these look
the same as our general um our

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.260
general formula from x when it's x to
the n so now when we take

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.930
h prime of x we

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.820
can take the 6 out just like we did in the top
example and then we're going to apply

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:01.720
the power rule to x to the negative 2 the power
of negative 2. When we apply the

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:07.060
power rule, we take negative 2 as our n.
So it's going to be negative 2

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:12.540
times x to the n minus 1. It's going
to be negative 2 minus 1,

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.780
right? Because negative 2 is our n, and we're
subtracting 1 just like how we take

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:20.480
the power rule with our formula. Same thing for
the next number. We're going to have

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:24.220
a negative 4 out. We're going to take that factor
out, and then we're going to bring

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.300
our negative 3 down from the power and
subtract 1 from our negative 3, so it's

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.700
negative 3 minus 1.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:35.580
We can now expand this, and we're going to
get negative 12x to the negative 3

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:39.580
plus 12, because since we have two negatives
here, it's going to cancel out, and then 4 times

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.480
3 is 12, and we have x to the negative 4.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:47.820
This is the answer when we take the derivative
of h [of] x, but if you also want,

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:52.060
you can put it in the same form we had originally
and we can make this instead negative

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.120
12 over x to the power of 3 plus 12

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.460
over x to the power of 4. And see
here we just brought,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:06.150
we use the identity again um for negative
powers just like we did at the beginning of

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.580
our example. On to our next one,
we have g of x equals

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:15.000
3 square root x plus 2 over the square
root of x. And at this point, probably, we're now

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.060
getting the hang of what we need to do before
we apply our power rule. We have to

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:24.380
put these x's in a form that match our f of
x equals x to the n form. So

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:29.610
here, what we can do is we can
rewrite g of x as g

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.020
of x equals 3 times x to the 1 over 2,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:40.280
because 1 over 2, if you take something to the 1
over 2 power it's the same as if you

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:45.770
take the square root and then we also have
x, root x down here so we can do 2

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:50.930
over x to the 1 over 2 and from the last
example we know if we have

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:55.190
powers in the bottom, we can make them a negative
power and put them back to the top so

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.530
this can equal 3x to the 1 over 2 plus

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.590
2x to the negative 1 over 2.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:09.350
Now since this form matches our f of x
equals x to the n form we can now

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.650
take the prime so we're going to do g
prime of x equals take

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.590
that 3 out and then apply our power rule to the x
1 over 2 it's going to be 1 over 2

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.180
x and then we have 1 over 2 minus 1

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:30.520
plus 2 and then we're going going to
have negative 1 over 2 x

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:35.720
negative 1 over 2 minus 1 and this matches our
power rule once again because look we

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:39.180
have 1 over 2 here it gets shifted down we
subtract 1 from it we have negative 1

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:43.470
over 2 here it gets shifted down we subtract 1
from it if we continue expanding this

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.550
we get 3 over 2 x to the
negative 1 over 2 minus

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.050
x to the negative 3 over 2. All we do is
just expand that 3 into this

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:58.070
bracket and apply the subtraction to our
power and same thing with the 2

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.670
this is now our full answer but if we want we
can put it back into the same form

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.910
as the in roots and in fractions so here

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:14.290
we know that we have 3 over 2 in front and we
have x to the negative 1 over 2 we

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:18.170
know x to the 1 over 2 is a square root and
you know the negative means in the

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:23.580
denominator so it's gonna be 3 over 2 square
root of x and then we're gonna do

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:28.860
minus and here we know it's negative so first of
all it has to be in the denominator and

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:33.970
we know we have a 2 in the denominator of the
power so it's gonna be a square root

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:37.240
and because we have a 3 in the
numerator it's gonna be x cubed

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:43.340
so here we have 3 over 2 times
square root of x

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:48.830
minus 1 over square root of x
cubed now on to our last

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:52.490
example I think we've gotten the hang of it by
now we've got to put it into this

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:57.990
form right here before we start applying
our power rule so for our

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.910
last example, to put our first term into the
form of x to the n, you have to

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:08.390
multiply this 4 and this 3, because a power
